WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.630 --> 00:00:04.920
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:10.900
After around 160 episodes into Two Minute
Papers, I think it is no secret to anyone

00:00:10.900 --> 00:00:16.270
that I am helplessly addicted to fluid simulations,
so you can already guess what this episode

00:00:16.270 --> 00:00:17.470
will be about.

00:00:17.470 --> 00:00:23.570
I bet you will be as spellbound by this beautiful
footage of wet sand simulations as I was when

00:00:23.570 --> 00:00:24.759
I've first seen it.

00:00:24.759 --> 00:00:29.640
Before you ask, yes, I have attempted to prepare
some slow-motion action too!

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:34.250
As you remember, simulating the motion of
fluids involves solving equations that tell

00:00:34.250 --> 00:00:38.070
us how the velocity and the pressure evolves
in time.

00:00:38.070 --> 00:00:43.620
Now, the 3D world we live in is a continuum,
and we cannot solve these quantities everywhere

00:00:43.620 --> 00:00:46.320
because that would take an infinite amount
of time.

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:51.340
To alleviate this, we can put a grid in our
virtual world and obtain these quantities

00:00:51.340 --> 00:00:53.450
only in these gridpoints.

00:00:53.450 --> 00:00:57.620
The higher the resolution the grid is, the
more realistic the animations are, but the

00:00:57.620 --> 00:01:00.740
computation time also scales quite poorly.

00:01:00.740 --> 00:01:05.729
It is really not a surprise that we have barely
seen any wet sand simulations in the visual

00:01:05.729 --> 00:01:07.539
effects industry so far.

00:01:07.539 --> 00:01:12.280
Here, we have an efficient algorithm to handle
these cases, and as you will see, this is

00:01:12.280 --> 00:01:18.180
not only extremely expensive to compute, but
nasty stability issues also arise.

00:01:18.180 --> 00:01:19.950
Have a look at this example here.

00:01:19.950 --> 00:01:23.770
These are sand simulations with different
cohesion values.

00:01:23.770 --> 00:01:28.979
Cohesion means the strength of intermolecular
forces that hold the material together.

00:01:28.979 --> 00:01:33.979
The higher cohesion is, the harder it is to
break the sand up, the bigger the clumps are.

00:01:33.979 --> 00:01:38.749
This is an important quantity for our simulation
because the higher the water saturation of

00:01:38.749 --> 00:01:41.670
this block of sand, the more cohesive it is.

00:01:41.670 --> 00:01:46.600
Now, if we try to simulate this effect with
traditional techniques on a coarse grid, we'll

00:01:46.600 --> 00:01:52.149
encounter a weird phenomenon: namely, the
longer our simulation runs, the larger the

00:01:52.149 --> 00:01:54.219
volume of the sand becomes.

00:01:54.219 --> 00:01:58.720
An excellent way to demonstrate this phenomenon
is using these hourglasses, where you can

00:01:58.720 --> 00:02:04.329
clearly see that after only a good couple
turns, the amount of sand within is significantly

00:02:04.329 --> 00:02:05.469
increased.

00:02:05.469 --> 00:02:09.830
This is particularly interesting, because
normally, in classical fluid simulations,

00:02:09.830 --> 00:02:15.180
if our grid resolution is insufficient, we
typically encounter water volume dissipation,

00:02:15.180 --> 00:02:19.860
which means that the total amount of mass
in the simulation decreases over time.

00:02:19.860 --> 00:02:25.270
Here, we have the exact opposite, like in
a magic trick, after every turn, the volume

00:02:25.270 --> 00:02:26.730
gets inflated.

00:02:26.730 --> 00:02:30.050
That's a really peculiar and no less challenging
problem.

00:02:30.050 --> 00:02:35.860
This issue can be alleviated by using a finer
grid, which is, as we know, extremely costly

00:02:35.860 --> 00:02:41.260
to compute, or, the authors proposed a volume
fixing method to take care of this without

00:02:41.260 --> 00:02:44.660
significantly increasing the execution time
of the algorithm.

00:02:44.660 --> 00:02:49.610
Make sure to have a look at the paper, which
is certainly my kind of paper: lots of beautiful

00:02:49.610 --> 00:02:54.530
physics and a study on how to solve these
equations so that we can obtain an efficient

00:02:54.530 --> 00:02:56.100
wet sand simulator.

00:02:56.100 --> 00:02:59.980
And also, don't forget, a fluid paper a day
keeps the obsessions away.

00:02:59.980 --> 00:03:03.000
In the meantime, a word about the Two Minute
Papers shirts.

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:08.310
I am always delighted to see you Fellow Scholars
sending over photos of yourselves proudly

00:03:08.310 --> 00:03:11.420
posing with your newly obtained shirts for
the series.

00:03:11.420 --> 00:03:14.080
Thanks so much and please, keep them coming!

00:03:14.080 --> 00:03:19.430
They are available through twominutepapers.com
for the US, and the EU and worldwide link

00:03:19.430 --> 00:03:21.630
is also available in the video description.

00:03:21.630 --> 00:03:25.920
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

